      "id": "verbosity_explain_water",
      "category": "verbosity_fingerprint",
      "check_type": "length_range",
      "passed": false,
      "errored": false,
      "error": null,
      "latency_ms": 2251.2,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 739,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical compound made up of two elements: **hydrogen and oxygen**. Its chemical formula is **$\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$**, meaning every single molecule of water consists of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom. \n\nHowever, water is much more than just a chemical formula. It is the defining substance of our planet and the fundamental prerequisite for life as we know it. \n\nHere is a breakdown of water in all its fascinating dimensions:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Physical Properties (The Weird One)\nWater is actually a very strange substance compared to most other liquids on Earth. \n* **The Three States:** Water is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states: **liquid** (oceans, rain), **solid** (ice, snow), and **gas** (steam, vapor).\n* **Ice Floats:** Almost all substances shrink and become denser when they freeze. Water, however, *expands* and becomes less dense when it turns into ice. This is why ice floats on water. If ice sank, lakes and oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Water is exceptionally good at dissolving other substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals). This property is why it can transport nutrients through our bodies and through the roots of plants.\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat before its temperature changes significantly. This is why oceans help regulate the climate of the entire planet, and why bodies of water stay cooler in the summer and warmer in the winter than the surrounding land.\n\n### 2. Water on Earth\n* **Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas.\n* **Scarcity of Fresh Water:** Despite being abundant, about **96.5% of Earth's water is salt water** in the oceans. Only about **2.5% is fresh water**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use (in lakes, rivers, and the atmosphere).\n* **The Water Cycle:** Water on Earth is constantly moving in a closed loop driven by the sun. It evaporates from oceans and lakes into the air, condenses into clouds, falls back to the earth as precipitation (rain or snow), and flows back to the oceans via rivers. \n\n### 3. Water and Life\n* **The Biological Necessity:** Life originated in the oceans, and all living organisms—from the tiniest bacteria to giant blue whales—rely on water. \n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. Our brains are about 73% water, our lungs about 83%, and even our bones are about 31% water. \n* **Function:** Water regulates our internal body temperature, lubricates joints, flushes out waste through urine and sweat, and acts as the medium for virtually all the chemical reactions that happen inside our cells. Without water, a human being can only survive for about 3 to 5 days.\n\n### Summary\nWater is the ultimate connector. Geologically, it shapes our continents and regulates our climate. Biologically, it is the canvas upon which life is painted. Philosophically and culturally, it has been revered since ancient times as a symbol of purity, renewal, and life itself."
